## Submit to CRAN

Now in a separate branch: `vX.Y-Z-cran`, put `$FLIBS` at end of last line in `src/Makevars.in`.

Test on Windows using Winbuilder: `https://win-builder.r-project.org/upload.aspx`. This will run `R CMD check` and also check installation of package.

Can also test on various OS using rhub (from within `packages/nimble`):

0. One time only, one may need to run `gitcreds::gitcreds_set()`
1. `rhub::rc_submit()`
2. choose platform(s) of interest
3. look online for results

In some cases one might want to choose one or more of the more specialized platforms, e.g., `rchk` for memory protection checks.
CRAN seems only to use UBSAN (undefined behavior sanitizer) checking, though Kurt Hornik indicated that he built R with ASAN (address sanitizer) and UBSAN.

Submit to CRAN at https://cran.r-project.org, via the "web form" link at bottom of page.

When submitting to CRAN, use this template, including noting anything done to fix the CRAN results problems (or omitting if there are none):

```
This release fixes the  `rchk` issue noted on the CRAN package check results page. 

A couple additional comments (repeated from previous submissions):

1) The size of the installed package exceeds 10 MB because

a) The size of our R code base is large.
b) We include libnimble.{a,so}, whose size can be quite variable across different operating systems.
c) We include a copies of the Eigen linear algebra software and the CppAD automatic differentiation library, as we use Eigen for linear algebra and CppAD for automatic differentiation in our generated C++ code.
d) We include a directory of example code and data used in the original BUGS software for MCMC. This is used in our testing and in examples.

2) Per a discussion between Duncan Temple Lang and Uwe Ligges (at 2016 DSC in Stanford), building binaries on CRAN for this package will use --merge-multiarch.
```

